WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.369
One of America's leading doctors

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.203
shocked the world

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:03.870
when at age

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.038
57, he announced

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:07.307
he only wanted to live to 75.

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.641
As he gets closer to that age,

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.477
is he sticking to his plan?

00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:12.912
In the October 2014 Atlantic article

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.348
Why I Hope to Die at 75, Dr.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.950
Ezekiel Emanuel

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.685
vowed that when he hit that age,

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.153
his approach to his health care

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.855
will completely change

00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:22.622
that while he won't

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:24.224
actively end his life,

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:26.226
he wouldn't try to prolong it.

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.327
He cited statistics

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.161
on how America

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:29.262
is living longer

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.296
and why that wasn't

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:32.098
necessarily a good thing.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.066
In 1900,

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:34.601
the life expectancy of an average

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:37.203
American was approximately 47 years.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.206
By 1990, it was over 75.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:41.875
Since his article appeared,

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:45.211
the number peaked in 2019 at nearly 79.

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.280
But due in part to COVID 19,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.615
it most recently was measured

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.917
at around 76 and a half. Dr.

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:53.186
Emanuel also showed this sobering chart

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:54.187
about how creative

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.688
productivity falls off

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:58.091
a cliff after one's early sixties.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:00:59.659
He points out that when parents

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:01.327
routinely live to 95,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:02.362
children must care

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.564
take into their own retirement.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.266
However, now it's more than eight years

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.801
later and he's 65

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.402
and looking pretty good by the way.

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.237
So how's he feeling about all of this?

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.173
Dr. Ezekiel Emanuel joins me now.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.307
He's an oncologist,

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:15.308
bioethicist,

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:16.509
a senior fellow at the Center

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:17.777
for American Progress.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.913
Also vice provost for global initiatives

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:22.015
at the University of Pennsylvania.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:22.882
Welcome back.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:26.519
Your title was Why I Hope to Die at 75.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.455
But really, what you were writing about

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:30.924
was quality of life, not just longevity.

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:31.357
Right.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:33.159
Yes.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:36.029
And your audience should remember that

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.997
authors do not give their articles,

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.432
titles, editors do.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:40.667
And editors have an interest

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.001
in pumping up

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.470
sales, not necessarily

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.672
in being totally accurate.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.273
Yeah. Look at 75.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.309
It's not a matter of

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.844
do I want medical interventions

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:53.079
if I happen to be skiing or something,

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.314
I would definitely take

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.116
medical interventions.

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:59.319
If I say broke my hip or injured

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:02.622
my knee, that's definitely the case.

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.991
And if I'm in serious pain,

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.093
I would take pain medications

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.828
The thing I was saying

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.997
is that what I don't want to do is say

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:12.165
take chemotherapy

00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:14.801
if I got cancer at that point.

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.137
And those are important distinctions.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:19.372
I'm not saying stop medical care,

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:20.907
which is what that

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:24.177
title and a lot of the language around

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:25.378
this. Okay.

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:25.945
But Dr.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:26.579
Emanuel, there

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:28.014
there are a lot of interventions.

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:29.482
I'm going to put them on the screen

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:30.450
that you say

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.584
when you hit 75,

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.252
you're no longer

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:33.686
interested in

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.222
regular preventive tests, screenings,

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.558
interventions, colon, icecaps

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.460
and other cancer screening tests,

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.461
cardiac stress

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:43.163
test, pacemaker, implantable

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:44.464
defibrillator, heart

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.533
valve replacement or bypass surgery,

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.601
flu shots or antibiotics.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:49.903
Ventilators, dialysis,

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:51.204
surgery, medication.

00:02:51.204 --> 00:02:53.239
God forbid if you get cancer,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:54.774
you're just going to let it ride

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:57.177
Yes, sir.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:58.778
And you know what?

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.346
You know, Michael,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.149
a lot of those interventions,

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.019
they're not necessarily

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.420
pleasant interventions,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.888
especially cancer, chemotherapy.

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:10.590
I'm an oncologist, as you mentioned,

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.592
and I know a lot about them.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.860
And I think

00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:16.095
the real question is not at that moment.

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.097
The real question is,

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:19.399
how do you want to live your life?

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.101
What is quality of life mean to you?

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.970
And, you know, I'm pretty clear

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.538
about what it means to me.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:26.806
And the reason I wrote that

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:29.042
article was to suggest

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.510
that people need to be clear

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.445
about what it means to them.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:33.646
I wasn't telling everyone

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:34.747
to do what I do.

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.783
I said, you need to think about it.

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.818
You just can't sort of blindly

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.353
go into old age,

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:40.853
which I think

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.422
unfortunately happens to many,

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:43.323
many people.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:45.124
And the medical system will do

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.826
what the medical system does,

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:47.760
regardless of what

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.629
your philosophy of life is.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.730
And what I was urging

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:51.798
people think about

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:52.932
what's important to you.

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:54.234
You know, what's important to me

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.702
is to be mentally active,

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:56.869
mentally engaged,

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:57.570
doing what

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.404
I can do

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.873
to make the world a better place.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:02.742
I want to be physically calm

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:05.411
and you wait I want to be with Dr.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:05.912
Emanuel.

00:04:07.614 --> 00:04:08.147
Yeah.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:11.050
Your father your father lived to 92.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.386
I think your mother is still with us.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.922
You were dealt a really good hand.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:19.759
You're still taking the under

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:22.829
And look, you're exactly right, Michael.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.597
I may turn out to be

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.733
what I call in the article, an outlier.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:27.600
Right.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.302
I was talking about, on average,

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:30.303
just because

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:32.171
not everyone can be an outlier,

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.007
almost all of us will be outliers.

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:35.908
But by definition, that's not true.

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:37.910
On average, there's a problem.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.979
And let me point out a statistic.

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:41.314
You mentioned at the top

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.348
that our average life

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:43.950
expectancy is going down.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.251
What that really means

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:46.319
is that a lot of young

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.488
people are not living to 75.

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:50.223
That's the real tragedy

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:52.625
that our system has not made

00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:53.793
everyone be able

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.962
to live a healthy life to 75.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.031
That's what we should be aiming for.

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:00.433
Living to a hundred or living to 90.

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:01.200
You know,

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.502
those extra years,

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:04.570
they're not in your thirties or forties,

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:05.905
they're in your nineties.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:06.906
And that's

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:10.310
not necessarily where people anticipate

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.312
that extra year or month

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:14.013
or whatever it's going to be.

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.716
And I'm pretty clear in my own mind

00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:17.684
about the fact

00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:19.952
that I want to be vigorous until the end.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.422
And that's what's important to me,

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:26.192
that the end is 75 or 80 or whatever

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.126
it's going to be.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.695
I think the tragedy is

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:30.163
we have too many Americans

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:31.898
not making it to 75.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:36.135
As you draw closer to 75,

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.170
is the Emanuel

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:38.938
clan lobbying

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:42.241
putting pressure on you to reconsider?

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:44.644
Look, I have I've had discussions

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:48.181
with my partner extensively about this.

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:48.915
If I'm

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:50.583
an outlier, as I think

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.652
I've said in public before.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:53.553
If I'm vigorous,

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:56.055
I like Tony Foushee still being active,

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:59.058
still making major contributions

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.259
into my eighties.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.328
You know that I'll have to reconsider.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.564
And I fully aware of that.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.599
I said that in the article

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:09.802
that 75 was a sort of average age.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.071
It's not what I mean.

00:06:12.071 --> 00:06:12.839
What's important

00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:15.508
is the quality that at that point

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.511
when you hit 90

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:19.912
I want to book the interview,

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:20.480
God willing,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.482
that I'm able to pull it off myself.

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:22.782
Okay.

00:06:22.782 --> 00:06:23.416
So can we,

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:24.784
can we put that in the date book

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:26.419
right now?

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:27.320
I'll be a law.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.322
I would be an amazing outlier

00:06:29.322 --> 00:06:31.557
if that were true.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.793
Michael, you know, there are outliers.

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:36.596
I just put up a Coursera

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:37.964
course on Ben Franklin.

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:41.167
He was an outlier way back in the 1700s

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:45.004
where at when he was, I think 82.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.072
He participated

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.074
in the Constitution convention

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:51.310
gave a very very famous and a speech

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:51.711
at the

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:53.312
end of the Constitutional Convention

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.580
that's worth looking at.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.916
He wrote part of his autobiography

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:57.650
at the end.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:58.918
He was very vigorous

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:00.987
all the way until 84 when he died.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:01.621
You know

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.256
God willing I should be like that.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:04.891
Dr.

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:05.625
Emanuel, thank you.

00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:07.126
I wish you good health

00:07:07.393 --> 00:07:08.995
Take care. Nice to be with you.

